      "content": "Ah, I see where you're coming from. In the world of water heaters, goblins are just a metaphor for the unexpected issues that can arise. Now, regarding the tapping noise that has picked up speed, it's possible that the issue is related to the water pump. If the pump is failing, it might be working harder to push water through the system, which could cause the tapping noise to become more pronounced and faster.\n\nAnother possibility is that the water heater is experiencing a problem with the thermostat. If the thermostat is malfunctioning, it might be causing the heating element to cycle on and off more frequently, which could result in a faster tapping noise.\n\nIt's also worth considering that the tapping noise could be a sign of sediment buildup at the bottom of the tank. If there's a lot of sediment, it could cause the water to slosh around more violently, leading to a faster tapping sound.\n\nTo get to the bottom of this, you'll want to inspect the water heater and its components. Check the water pump, thermostat, and heating element to see if any of them are faulty. If you're not comfortable doing this yourself, it's always a good idea to call in a professional plumber to help you diagnose and fix the issue.\n\nRemember, when dealing with",
